README.OSK -- how this tree differs from comp.sources.games volume 8's castleORIG/ is the posting exactly as comp.sources.games carried it, volume 8issues 93 to 97: The Realm of the Wizard, Ted Wisniewski's first-persondungeon game (1990), with Bill Randle's changes as moderator.  ORIG/FILES isthe game's data, which is on the disk in /dd/GAMES/CASTLE with an emptyscores file and a SAVE directory.  ORIG/patches01 is Patch1 (volume 9 issue31, February 1990), Bill Randle's fix for char arrays that were overrun,"causing lockup and core dumps".  This tree has Patch1 applied, andINCLUDE/patchlevel.h is the file it adds.The game itself is unchanged.  It builds with GAME_HOURS, as the Makefiledoes; the shipped cas_hours keeps every hour open.  Every change is forOS-9's compiler, linker, terminal or files:  INCLUDE/castle.h, items.h, windows.h               l68 has no common symbols, so a global defined in a header               clashes in every file that includes it.  CASTLE_EXTERN is               extern except in castle.c, which defines it first.  The data               paths are written out in full as /dd/GAMES/CASTLE/... in               place of the Makefile's -DFILES quote trick, and the save               directory is /dd/GAMES/CASTLE/SAVE/ in place of /usr/tmp.               screens[] is declared extern here; screens.c defines it.  INCLUDE/monst.h               the same, as MONST_EXTERN, defined in monster.c.  The monster               pictures are compiled only into monster.c, which draws them:               magic.c includes the header too, and a second copy of the               pictures took castle past l68's 64K of static data.  screens.c, monst.h               the pictures are char, not short.  Every value is a printing               character (12 to 126) and both are drawn with %c, so the               screens are the same; as short they needed twice the static               data.  castle.c     no <sys/time.h>; each signal() and case label for a signal               OS-9's <signal.h> does not name (ILL, SEGV, TERM, FPE, BUS)               is left out.  files.c      no <sys/time.h>.  LF is '\n', OS-9 C's CR, in place of               '\012': the help pager counts the help file's lines by it,               and on this disk they end in CR.  Counting LF it never               paged, and `castle -h' stopped at the foot of the first               screen.  store.c      ask_ques() declares char word[80]; c68 will not take an               array with no size.  tty.c        new: the game's savetty() and resetty(), which castle.h               renames to tty_save() and tty_reset() -- curses.l has its               own of those names, which keep the options but change none               of them.  While the game runs these clear three of the               terminal's special characters and afterwards restore them.               OS-9's driver turns ^C and ^E into signals, and a signal               aborts the read curses is waiting in: ^C hung the game, and               ^E, its save key, stopped it with nothing saved.  And ESC,               the only key that leaves the inventory, is the driver's               end-of-file character, so getch() returned -1 for it and the               inventory could not be left.  Now all three arrive as keys,               as under Unix: ^E saves and leaves, ESC closes the               inventory, and ^C, which the game had as "Really Quit?", is               an ordinary key -- `q' asks that question.The README's set-uid install does not apply: the save files and scores arepublicly writable, as every data file on this disk is.